      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Previous problem: https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3216913h3170956_nxn_rational\nTo find the range of the function \\(f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\), we start by using a known trigonometric identity for the sum of arctangents. Specifically, for any real number \\(y\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\arctan y + \\arctan \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\quad \\text{if } y > 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\arctan y + \\arctan \\frac{1}{y} = 0 \\quad \\text{if } y < -1.\n\\]\n\nApplying this identity to our function, we set \\(y = x\\), so our function becomes:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}.\n\\]\n\nWe substitute \\(y = x\\) into the identity:\n\n\\[\n\\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\quad \\text{if } x > 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = 0 \\quad \\text{if } x < -1.\n\\]\n\nThis means that \\(f(x)\\) can only take the values \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) and \\(0\\). To confirm, we can check the behavior of \\(f(x)\\) at the boundaries and within the intervals. At \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -1\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nf(1) = \\arctan 1 + \\arctan 0 = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 0 = \\frac{\\pi}{4},\n\\]\n\\[\nf(-1) = \\arctan (-1) + \\arctan \\left(\\frac{1 - (-1)}{1 + (-1)}\\right) = \\arctan (-1) + \\arctan 2 = -\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 0.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\in (-1, 1)\\), the expression inside the arctan function \\(\\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\) is biased towards the standard -1 > -1 case, so:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\arctan \\left(\\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}\\right) = -\\frac{\\pi}{4},\n\\]\n\nfor \\(x \\in (-1, 1)\\).\n\nThus, the function \\(f(x)\\) can only take the values \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\). Therefore, the range of \\(f(x)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n\\]",
